1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,169
Can you show me the attack surface of a container?

2
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:15,863
The attack surface is everything an attacker could use once they get code running in your container:

3
00:00:15,863 --> 00:00:18,858
a shell, a package manager, network tools,

4
00:00:18,858 --> 00:00:25,917
setuid programs, the root user, Linux capabilities, a writable file system, access to host devices.

5
00:00:25,917 --> 00:00:29,482
Each lesson of this module removes one part of it.

6
00:00:29,482 --> 00:00:35,756
This lesson shows you how to see the attack surface, and the most dangerous single flag:

7
00:00:35,756 --> 00:00:36,683
--privileged.

8
00:00:38,433 --> 00:00:44,256
How many programs ship in a general-purpose base image, compared with a small one?

9
00:00:44,256 --> 00:00:47,593
Every program is something an attacker can use.

10
00:00:48,133 --> 00:00:51,538
Every program is something an attacker can use.

11
00:00:51,538 --> 00:00:53,929
Some are more useful than others:

12
00:00:53,929 --> 00:01:02,911
a package manager installs more tools, and setuid programs run with their owner's rights (usually root) whoever starts them:

13
00:01:02,911 --> 00:01:05,230
A distroless image goes further:

14
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:10,518
no shell, no package manager, only the application and its runtime files.

15
00:01:11,067 --> 00:01:13,513
A distroless image goes further:

16
00:01:13,513 --> 00:01:19,093
no shell, no package manager, only the application and its runtime files.

17
00:01:19,093 --> 00:01:21,921
There is nothing to run a shell with:

18
00:01:23,667 --> 00:01:25,082
Let me try something.

19
00:01:25,082 --> 00:01:30,811
A teammate cannot get a tool to work in a container and "fixes" it with --privileged.

20
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:39,517
The privileged container sees every device of the host (disks included) and has every capability (CapEff with all bits set):

21
00:01:39,517 --> 00:01:45,499
with the host's disk device, it can mount the host's file system and change anything on it.

22
00:01:45,499 --> 00:01:47,931
The kernel settings are writable too:

23
00:01:49,667 --> 00:01:51,150
Remove --privileged.

24
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:59,014
Find out which permission the tool actually needs and grant only that (a single capability with --cap-add,

25
00:01:59,014 --> 00:02:02,426
lesson 085, or a single device with --device):

26
00:02:04,167 --> 00:02:04,880
Got it.

27
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:07,631
What do I need to remember?

28
00:02:08,167 --> 00:02:14,031
The attack surface is every tool, permission and resource available inside the container.

29
00:02:14,567 --> 00:02:22,971
Fewer programs, no setuid binaries, no shell (distroless) = less for an attacker to use.

30
00:02:23,533 --> 00:02:28,096
--privileged gives the container all capabilities and all host devices:

31
00:02:28,096 --> 00:02:30,538
never use it to "make something work".

32
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:36,129
I keep hearing about running as a non-root user.

33
00:02:36,129 --> 00:02:37,645
What is it, really?

34
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:44,110
Unless the image says otherwise, the process in a container runs as root (user ID 0).

35
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:47,725
It is the same user ID 0 that the host kernel knows:

36
00:02:47,725 --> 00:02:50,715
if the process breaks out of the container,

37
00:02:50,715 --> 00:02:55,581
through a kernel bug or a careless bind mount, it is root on the host.

38
00:02:55,581 --> 00:03:00,240
The USER instruction makes the application run as an ordinary user.

39
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,960
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

