The broken version adds a user and USER app, and nothing else.

46
00:03:31,867 --> 00:03:35,117
The container exited with status 1.

47
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:36,696
Its logs say why:

48
00:03:38,433 --> 00:03:43,889
Give the user only the folder it writes to; the code stays owned by root,

49
00:03:43,889 --> 00:03:48,223
so even this user cannot modify it (see fixed/Dockerfile):

50
00:03:49,967 --> 00:03:50,680
Got it.

51
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,431
What do I need to remember?

52
00:03:53,967 --> 00:04:01,471
Containers run as root (uid 0) by default, the same uid 0 as on the host kernel.

53
00:04:02,033 --> 00:04:06,008
USER makes the application run as an unprivileged user.

54
00:04:06,567 --> 00:04:11,351
"Permission denied" after adding USER means the user has nowhere to write:

55
00:04:11,351 --> 00:04:13,096
chown only the data folder.

56
00:04:14,833 --> 00:04:17,121
How does image security:

57
00:04:17,121 --> 00:04:20,838
trusted, minimal, pinned, scanned work?

58
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:29,429
Your image is only as secure as everything inside it, and most of what is inside comes from the base image.

59
00:04:29,429 --> 00:04:31,981
Four habits keep it under control:

60
00:04:31,981 --> 00:04:32,131
1.

61
00:04:32,131 --> 00:04:32,731
Trusted:

62
00:04:32,731 --> 00:04:38,509
start from official images or verified publishers, never a random repository.

63
00:04:38,509 --> 00:04:38,659
2.

64
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:40,951
Minimal.

65
00:04:40,951 --> 00:04:46,533
The same language runtime in different variants (sizes as stored on this engine):

66
00:04:46,533 --> 00:04:50,254
The distroless image has no shell to look around with:

67
00:04:50,254 --> 00:04:57,835
list its files from the outside instead, with docker export (the file system of a container, as a tar stream).

68
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:02,271
The distroless image has no shell to look around with:

69
00:05:02,271 --> 00:05:10,156
list its files from the outside instead, with docker export (the file system of a container, as a tar stream).

70
00:05:10,156 --> 00:05:13,381
The container is only created, never started,

71
00:05:13,381 --> 00:05:20,549
so the command given to docker create (nothing-to-run, the image has no default command) never runs:

72
00:05:20,549 --> 00:05:22,270
What a scanner looks at.

73
00:05:22,833 --> 00:05:24,800
What a scanner looks at.

74
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,833
Every package with its exact version.

75
00:05:29,567 --> 00:05:31,102
Let me try something.

76
00:05:31,102 --> 00:05:35,856
Copy a digest from a chat message and lose half of it on the way:

77
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,557
invalid reference format:

78
00:05:38,557 --> 00:05:41,922
Docker did not even contact a registry.

79
00:05:41,922 --> 00:05:45,977
A digest reference must be exactly name@sha256:

80
00:05:45,977 --> 00:05:49,255
followed by 64 hexadecimal characters.

81
00:05:49,255 --> 00:05:50,204
Count them:

82
00:05:51,967 --> 00:05:55,930
Read the digest from the image (or from the registry,

83
00:05:55,930 --> 00:06:00,491
docker buildx imagetools inspect IMAGE) and use it unchanged:

84
00:06:02,233 --> 00:06:02,947
Got it.

85
00:06:02,947 --> 00:06:05,698
What do I need to remember?

86
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:14,928
Use official or verified base images; prefer the smallest variant that works (-slim, alpine, distroless).

87
00:06:15,467 --> 00:06:19,039
A tag can move; a digest (@sha256:

88
00:06:19,039 --> 00:06:24,186
+ 64 hex characters) always means the same bytes.

89
00:06:24,733 --> 00:06:32,573
Scanners compare the installed package versions against CVE databases; rebuild on fixed base images regularly.

90
00:06:34,333 --> 00:06:37,043
Why would I need secrets in images?

91
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:41,890
Anyone who can pull an image can read everything in it:

92
00:06:41,890 --> 00:06:45,635
every layer, the build history and the metadata.

93
00:06:45,635 --> 00:06:53,123
A secret that reached the image in any of these ways is leaked, even if a later step deletes it:

94
00:06:53,123 --> 00:06:57,258
ARG TOKEN used in a RUN step → stored in the history,

95
00:06:57,258 --> 00:07:01,236
ENV PASSWORD=… → stored in the image configuration,

96
00:07:01,236 --> 00:07:07,164
COPY credentials … then RUN rm … → still in the layer that the COPY created.

97
00:07:07,164 --> 00:07:07,788
The fix:

98
00:07:07,788 --> 00:07:13,717
build secrets (RUN --mount=type=secret) for secrets needed during the build,

99
00:07:13,717 --> 00:07:19,880
and runtime configuration for secrets the application needs (lessons 060, 061).

100
00:07:21,633 --> 00:07:25,593
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

