114
00:08:22,767 --> 00:08:23,480
Got it.

115
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:26,231
What do I need to remember?

116
00:08:26,767 --> 00:08:33,041
Anything in an image's layers, history or configuration is readable by everyone who can pull it.

117
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:40,255
ARG values used in RUN appear in docker history; ENV values appear in docker inspect.

118
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:45,335
Deleting a file in a later layer does not remove it from the earlier layer.

119
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:50,514
What should I know about read-only file systems?

120
00:08:51,067 --> 00:08:54,341
By default every container has a writable layer:

121
00:08:54,341 --> 00:08:57,820
a process can change any file it has permission to,

122
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:02,255
including the application's own code, configuration and binaries.

123
00:09:02,255 --> 00:09:10,236
An attacker who gets in can therefore install tools or plant a backdoor that survives until the container is removed.

124
00:09:10,236 --> 00:09:14,466
--read-only makes the container's whole file system read-only.

125
00:09:14,466 --> 00:09:19,855
The few places the application really writes to become tmpfs mounts (in memory,

126
00:09:19,855 --> 00:09:22,106
empty at every start) or volumes.

127
